WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.770
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:17.167
Unit fifteen: Science and Society. In this unit, you will discuss how science progresses and serves society.

4
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:19.927
scientific revolution.

5
00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:25.889
scientific revolution means a period of fundamental change in scientific thinking.

6
00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:30.093
The scientific revolution transformed our view of the universe.

7
00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:34.444
Your turn. Say: scientific revolution.

8
00:00:37.144 --> 00:00:38.865
scientific revolution.

9
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.130
paradigm shift.

10
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.639
paradigm shift means a fundamental change in the basic assumptions of a field.

11
00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:51.214
Einstein's relativity caused a paradigm shift in physics.

12
00:00:51.914 --> 00:00:55.100
Your turn. Say: paradigm shift.

13
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.150
paradigm shift.

14
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.685
research ethics.

15
00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:07.229
research ethics means moral principles guiding scientific research.

16
00:01:07.529 --> 00:01:11.363
Research ethics require informed consent from participants.

17
00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:15.749
Your turn. Say: research ethics.

18
00:01:18.449 --> 00:01:19.682
research ethics.

19
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:21.870
open science.

20
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:27.948
open science means making research data and results freely available.

21
00:01:28.248 --> 00:01:30.584
Open science speeds up discovery.

22
00:01:31.284 --> 00:01:34.169
Your turn. Say: open science.

23
00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:38.160
open science.

24
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.697
citizen science.

25
00:01:41.397 --> 00:01:45.382
citizen science means research involving members of the public.

26
00:01:45.682 --> 00:01:49.040
Birdwatchers contribute to citizen science projects.

27
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.100
Your turn. Say: citizen science.

28
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.254
citizen science.

29
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.017
scientific integrity.

30
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:05.921
scientific integrity means honesty and rigour in conducting research.

31
00:02:06.221 --> 00:02:09.637
Falsifying data violates scientific integrity.

32
00:02:10.337 --> 00:02:14.081
Your turn. Say: scientific integrity.

33
00:02:16.781 --> 00:02:18.560
scientific integrity.

34
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.050
conflict of interest.

35
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:27.651
conflict of interest means a situation where personal gain may influence judgement.

36
00:02:27.951 --> 00:02:31.158
Researchers must declare any conflict of interest.

37
00:02:31.858 --> 00:02:35.439
Your turn. Say: conflict of interest.

38
00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:39.721
conflict of interest.

39
00:02:40.717 --> 00:02:42.171
peer review process.

40
00:02:42.871 --> 00:02:48.934
peer review process means evaluation of research by independent experts before publication.

41
00:02:49.234 --> 00:02:51.732
The peer review process took six months.

42
00:02:52.432 --> 00:02:56.199
Your turn. Say: peer review process.

43
00:02:58.899 --> 00:03:00.342
peer review process.

44
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.535
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:03.835 --> 00:03:09.738
Rithy, Thomas Kuhn argued that science advances through paradigm shifts. Do you agree?

46
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:16.953
Largely. The word 'revolution' carries real weight there; it isn't just steady progress.

47
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:23.921
Interesting choice of word. 'Revolution' has a much stronger connotation than 'development'.

48
00:03:25.217 --> 00:03:30.330
Exactly. Copernicus didn't merely refine astronomy; he overturned it.

49
00:03:31.617 --> 00:03:34.290
What threatens scientific integrity today?

50
00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:41.428
Conflicts of interest, for one. When industry funds research, doubts naturally arise.

51
00:03:42.717 --> 00:03:44.101
And open science?

52
00:03:45.417 --> 00:03:49.404
It sheds light on hidden methods. Transparency breeds trust.

53
00:03:50.717 --> 00:03:53.993
Grammar focus: Collocation and connotation.

54
00:03:54.293 --> 00:03:58.963
At C2, collocation and connotation make your language precise.

55
00:03:59.263 --> 00:04:04.377
The word carries real weight. Open science sheds light on hidden methods.

56
00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:08.279
Revolution has a stronger connotation than development.

57
00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:17.637
At C2, choose words for their connotations as well as their meanings: 'stubborn' and 'determined' describe similar behaviour very differently.

58
00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.860
Now read and listen. How Science Changes.

59
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:27.038
Philosopher Thomas Kuhn argued that science does not progress in a straight line.

60
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:34.076
Instead, long periods of normal science are interrupted by scientific revolutions.

61
00:04:35.383 --> 00:04:40.448
During a paradigm shift, the basic assumptions of a field are overturned.

62
00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.548
The move from an Earth-centred to a Sun-centred universe is a classic example.

63
00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:55.225
Today, science faces new challenges to its credibility, from conflicts of interest to failures of replication.

64
00:04:56.517 --> 00:05:02.696
The peer review process, though imperfect, remains a cornerstone of scientific integrity.

65
00:05:03.983 --> 00:05:10.325
Open science and citizen science are helping to make research more transparent and inclusive.

66
00:05:11.617 --> 00:05:16.345
Trust in science, it seems, must be earned through openness rather than authority.

67
00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.349
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.649 --> 00:05:24.494
A fundamental change in a field's assumptions is a blank.

69
00:05:24.794 --> 00:05:30.677
A, paradigm shift. B, conflict of interest. C, citizen science.

70
00:05:34.683 --> 00:05:37.310
The answer is A: paradigm shift.

71
00:05:38.817 --> 00:05:41.060
The evidence blank light on the problem.

72
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.503
A, sheds. B, gives. C, makes.

73
00:05:48.517 --> 00:05:50.992
The answer is A: sheds.

74
00:05:52.483 --> 00:05:55.609
Which word has the most positive connotation?

75
00:05:55.909 --> 00:06:00.178
A, stubborn. B, determined. C, obstinate.

76
00:06:04.183 --> 00:06:06.531
The answer is B: determined.

77
00:06:08.017 --> 00:06:12.396
Evaluation of research by independent experts is the blank.

78
00:06:12.696 --> 00:06:17.813
A, peer review process. B, research ethics. C, open science.

79
00:06:21.817 --> 00:06:24.675
The answer is A: peer review process.

80
00:06:26.183 --> 00:06:29.239
According to Kuhn, how does science progress?

81
00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:36.188
A, in a straight line. B, through scientific revolutions. C, randomly.

82
00:06:40.183 --> 00:06:43.657
The answer is B: through scientific revolutions.

83
00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:50.345
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can the public's trust in science be strengthened?

84
00:06:55.645 --> 00:07:01.302
Part two: Use three collocations and comment on the connotation of one key word.

85
00:07:07.602 --> 00:07:21.655
Open science sheds light on methods, and declaring conflicts of interest breeds trust. Scientists should also avoid the word 'proof' in public; it carries a connotation of finality that science rarely claims.

86
00:07:21.955 --> 00:07:24.442
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:30.355
Great work today! You learned eight new words and collocation and connotation.

88
00:07:30.655 --> 00:07:34.155
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:34.455 --> 00:07:37.316
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:37.616 --> 00:07:40.059
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

